.lua
book.chapter(33, "Embedding in D and through the C ABI")
.endlua
.sh 2 "D follows the same language contract"
.pp
The D implementation is imported as MetaML and constructs Session with new.
Native allocation and delegate conventions differ from C++, but the accepted
MetaML source and observable results should agree. Both snake_case methods
and several camelCase aliases exist; this book uses the shared snake_case
style where possible. Explicitly load the library tree and inspect Results
just as in the C++ client rather than assuming the prelude has been loaded
by constructing a native object.
.lua
book.code("d", [=[import MetaML;
import std.stdio : writeln, stderr;
int main(string[] args) {
    auto session = new Session;
    auto loaded = session.load_stdlib(args.length > 1 ? args[1] : "metalib");
    if (loaded.is_err()) {
        foreach (d; loaded.unwrap_err()) stderr.write(session.render(d));
        return 1;
    }
    auto result = session.eval("List.sum [10, 20, 12]");
    if (result.is_err()) {
        foreach (d; result.unwrap_err()) stderr.write(session.render(d));
        return 1;
    }
    writeln(to_string(result.unwrap()));
    return 0;
}]=], "The D counterpart of the complete C++ client")
book.code("sh", [=[ldc2 -i -I. embedding.d -of=embedding-d
./embedding-d metalib]=], "LDC compiles imported modules with -i")
.endlua
.pp
Typed registration supports D numeric types, bool, string, Nullable, arrays,
tuples, and Value according to the implementation's conversion traits.
Low-level callbacks receive PrimContext and a Value array. Recoverable D
Exceptions become M0409 when invoked through the primitive boundary, while
language MetaError diagnostics preserve their original code. Defects and
process faults are not ordinary recoverable script input.
.sh 2 "Garbage collection does not erase ownership"
.pp
Garbage collection simplifies retention of language objects, but native
resources still need explicit lifetime rules. A handle can own a file,
buffer, or library-specific object whose cleanup policy is not adequately
described by "some memory is reachable." Closures can retain environments,
and callbacks can retain host delegates. Review cycles and resource cleanup
when embedding in a long-running application rather than a short command-line
process.
.pp
Install the portable host bundle through metalib.d.Metalib and installAll,
then configure the same allowlist used by the corresponding C++ application.
Parity testing should compare primitive names, types, values, errors, output,
and canonical syntax results. It should not compare native object layouts
or assume identical allocation counts. Keep dialect-specific conveniences
below the language boundary so a portable script does not depend on its host.
.sh 2 "The C adapter is a different native boundary"
.pp
The genuine C99 interface lives in metalib/c/MetaML.h and is backed by a
separately compiled C++20 adapter. Link the metaml-c target and the C++
runtime. The .c files under metalib/cpp are unrelated included C++ fragments;
they must not be compiled as C. A C client receives an opaque session pointer,
status codes, and owned text results instead of native C++ Value objects.
.lua
book.code("c", [=[#include "metalib/c/MetaML.h"
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
int main(void) {
    metaml_c_session *session = NULL;
    metaml_text text = {0};
    const char *source = "List.sum [10, 20, 12]";
    int status = 1;
    if (metaml_session_create(&session) != METAML_OK) return 1;
    if (metaml_c_load_stdlib(session, "metalib") != METAML_OK) goto done;
    if (metaml_c_eval(session, source, strlen(source), &text) != METAML_OK)
        goto done;
    fwrite(text.data, 1, text.size, stdout);
    status = 0;
done:
    if (status) fputs(metaml_last_error(session), stderr);
    metaml_text_free(&text);
    metaml_session_destroy(session);
    return status;
}]=], "A complete C99 client with explicit ownership")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Owned bytes and borrowed diagnostics"
.pp
Initialize metaml_text to zero, release it with metaml_text_free, and free
the previous result before reusing the structure as an output argument.
The adapter owns the allocation convention; using an unrelated allocator's
free function is not the contract. Size excludes the terminating NUL, while
embedded zero bytes remain valid content. Use counted output such as fwrite
rather than assuming every result is ordinary zero-terminated text.
.pp
Metaml_last_error returns borrowed diagnostic text valid until the next
mutating operation or session destruction. Copy it if another subsystem must
retain it. Failed output-producing calls clear the output structure. Status
codes distinguish invalid arguments, language failures, and host failures.
No C++ exception crosses the ABI. These ownership and failure rules should
be tested from a translation unit compiled as C, not merely from C++ code
that happens to include the C header.
.sh 2 "Separate evaluated values from captured output"
.pp
Metaml_c_eval returns canonical MetaML notation. A string result therefore
includes the language's quoting and escaping rather than raw payload bytes.
Metaml_output copies captured ordinary output and can clear it after a
successful copy. Error-stream output and input retain process-stream behavior.
Type_of checks without evaluation, which is useful when a client needs a
type query without deliberately running the script's effects.
.pp
Fresh C sessions contain core primitives. Install the portable host bundle
with metaml_install_metalib and grant selected names through
metaml_allow_primitives. Installation does not grant access. The initial
adapter has no C callback-registration API; applications needing that level
of extension should use a C++ integration layer. The metaml_c_session type
also must not be confused with session pointers from the older optional
CBridge interface; their ABIs are distinct.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Keep an ownership table in the host binding: sessions are destroyed, result
buffers are freed, and borrowed diagnostic pointers are copied when retained.
This small inventory prevents many integration errors before testing begins.
.pp
Read MetaML.d, metalib/d/Metalib.d, metalib/c/MetaML.h, and MetaML.cpp in the
C adapter directory. Compile both complete clients where toolchains are
available. For C, test an error followed by a successful call and free every
owned result along both paths. Add a binary-output case containing a zero
byte, then explain why strlen is appropriate for the fixed source literal
in the example but not for arbitrary returned output.
